VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-025
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of bilateral round ligament pain causing an inability to walk and severe anxiety regarding preterm labor at 22 weeks.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a sudden onset of sharp, stabbing bilateral lower abdominal pain in a 22-week pregnant patient, causing her to double over and become unable to walk. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out acute placental abruption, preterm labor, or appendicitis, and to provide reassurance and mechanical support strategies to prevent an unnecessary, highly distressed emergency department presentation before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I stood up from the couch and suddenly got this tearing, stabbing pain down both sides of my stomach, and I am terrified I am going into early labor.
History of Present Illness: The patient is currently 22 weeks pregnant, with a previously normal antenatal course including secondary amenorrhoea, resolved first-trimester nausea, and progressive symmetric enlargement of the uterus. She reports maternal perception of fetal movements (quickening) starting at 19 weeks. Tonight, upon abruptly standing, she experienced sudden, sharp, shooting pains bilaterally in the lower abdomen and groin. The pain is exacerbated by any movement, particularly rolling over in bed or walking, and improves with rest. She specifically denies vaginal bleeding, fluid leakage, regular rhythmic uterine contractions, or dysuria.
Functional Status: Confined to the couch, afraid to move and trigger the sharp pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone currently; acute, severe anxiety regarding the safety of the pregnancy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious, lying very still on her side, holding her lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is gravid, consistent with a 22-week gestation. Uterus is soft, non-tender to direct central palpation, and completely relaxed (no palpable contractions). Exquisite, superficial, band-like tenderness is elicited upon palpation of the lateral margins of the uterus extending down into the inguinal canals bilaterally (classic round ligament distribution). Fetal heart tones are auscultated via Doppler at 150 bpm, regular rhythm. No vaginal bleeding observed.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prenatal vitamins.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Bilateral Round Ligament Spasm/Pain (Normal physiological pregnancy change) - Status: Acute flare
Differentials:

Preterm Labor - Ruled out by the complete absence of regular, rhythmic uterine contractions, lack of pelvic pressure, and normal fetal heart rate.

Placental Abruption - Ruled out by the soft, non-tender central uterus (no "woody" or rigid uterus) and the complete absence of vaginal bleeding.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out by the bilateral nature of the pain, its clear relation to movement/posture, and the lack of systemic fever or gastrointestinal symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly distressing pain presentation during the mid-trimester. Objective clinical examination confirmed normal fetal heart tones, a soft, non-contracting uterus, and pain strictly localized to the anatomical distribution of the round ligaments. This definitively ruled out catastrophic obstetric emergencies (abruption, preterm labor) that the patient acutely feared. The sudden, severe nature of the sharp pain causing functional immobility and profound psychological distress required urgent after-hours evaluation to safely diagnose a benign, physiological stretching mechanism and prevent an unnecessary, resource-intensive transfer to a tertiary maternity ward.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Confirmed normal fetal heart tones with the patient, providing immediate psychological reassurance. Administered oral paracetamol 1g for immediate analgesia. Demonstrated and applied a rudimentary abdominal support using a wide, elasticated bandage to immediately lift the weight of the uterus off the ligaments.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent the sudden stretching of the ligaments, strictly practice the "log roll" technique when getting out of bed, keeping your knees together and rolling entirely onto your side before pushing up; experiencing the absence of the sharp spasm will reinforce this new mechanical movement. To provide continuous mechanical relief, wear a dedicated maternity support belt whenever standing or walking; feeling the weight lifted off your pelvis will ensure daily compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Maternity belly band/support belt highly recommended.
Follow-up: Routine antenatal care with the primary obstetrician as scheduled; no urgent follow-up required for this specific complaint.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department or local maternity assessment unit if you develop regular, painful cramps that wrap around your back, if you experience any vaginal bleeding or a gush of clear fluid, or if the baby stops moving.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This pain, while severe, is a completely normal physiological consequence of the uterus rapidly growing and stretching its supporting cables.
Clinical Course: The sharp spasms will likely occur intermittently throughout the second trimester, specifically when you move suddenly, sneeze, or cough. Paracetamol and physical support are the mainstays of treatment.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate obstetric care if you experience actual rhythmic contractions, bleeding, or fluid leakage.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The round ligaments are like thick ropes that hold your uterus in place. Because your baby is growing rapidly at 22 weeks, these ropes are stretched incredibly tight. When you stood up quickly, they spasmed, causing that terrifying, sharp, tearing pain. Your baby is perfectly safe, your uterus is soft, and you are not in labor. The support bandage mimics the action of your muscles, taking the strain off those ligaments.
Actionable Steps: Move slowly when changing positions. Order a proper maternity support belt tomorrow, and take paracetamol if the aching persists tonight.

